    title: 'The Sociology of Scientific Knowledge',
    passageText: `The sociology of scientific knowledge (SSK) emerged in the 1970s as a radical challenge to the traditional view that science generates knowledge through a straightforward process of observation, hypothesis, and rational evaluation. The Edinburgh School's "strong programme," articulated by David Bloor, argued that sociological explanation should apply symmetrically to all knowledge claims — true and false, successful and failed scientific programs alike. Rather than explaining successful science by its rationality and failed science by social factors, SSK demanded that the same causal factors — interests, social organization, material practices — explain both.

This symmetry principle was deeply controversial. Critics, particularly philosophers of science, argued that it committed a category error: social conditions explain why scientists come to hold beliefs, but they cannot explain why those beliefs are true. The causes of belief-formation and the justification for belief-content are logically independent. Rationalist critics held that sound scientific method — controlled experiment, peer review, mathematical formalism — provides genuine access to how the world is, regardless of the social circumstances of its practitioners.

Harry Collins's empirical program of relativism (EPOR) extended SSK by studying ongoing scientific controversies, arguing that "interpretive flexibility" characterizes genuine scientific disputes: different experimental results can support different theoretical interpretations, and closure on a controversy is achieved through social negotiation rather than compelling evidence alone. His study of gravitational wave detection in the 1970s showed that physicists evaluated the same data differently based on their theoretical commitments and institutional affiliations.

The debate remains unresolved. A middle position, associated with laboratory studies (Latour and Woolgar's work on Salk Institute scientists), holds that scientific facts are produced through material practices and inscription devices, and that the distinction between "nature" and "social construction" is itself constructed in the course of laboratory work — without denying that the resulting knowledge is reliable and effective.`,
