    title: 'Memory, Identity, and Narrative Self',
    passageText: `The relationship between memory and personal identity has been a central question in philosophy since Locke, who argued that personal identity consists in continuity of consciousness — specifically, the ability to extend memory backward in time to connect present and past selves. On Locke's view, if I can remember performing an action, I am the same person who performed it; if I cannot, I am not identical with that earlier person for purposes of moral responsibility.

This view faces a series of objections. The "transitivity problem" arises because memory chains can be non-transitive: a person A (at time 3) may remember being B (at time 2), and B may have remembered being C (at time 1), yet A cannot remember being C. If identity is constituted by memory connections, then A is identical with B, B is identical with C, but A is not identical with C — violating the transitivity of identity. Thomas Reid raised this objection in the form of the "brave officer paradox."

Derek Parfit's influential work reconceives personal identity by distinguishing identity from what he calls "psychological connectedness" — overlapping chains of memory, intention, belief, and desire that connect persons over time. For Parfit, strict numerical identity (the "one true self" through time) matters less than the degree of psychological continuity. Parfit's radical conclusion is that personal identity is not what matters: what matters is psychological continuity, which can come in degrees and can survive even without strict identity.

Paul Ricoeur takes a different approach, arguing that the self is constituted through narrative. Rather than treating the self as a metaphysical substance or a chain of psychological states, Ricoeur distinguishes idem-identity (sameness — the body, the biological substrate that persists) from ipse-identity (selfhood — the narrative capacity that integrates past, present, and anticipated future into a coherent self-understanding). Narrative identity is not found but constructed — and it is this construction that makes us capable of keeping promises and being held responsible.`,
